version 1

// STOP AND REVERSE: always in the market, turning round on each signal.
//
// THE SIZE IS WHAT MAKES ONE CALL ENOUGH, and getting it wrong is the trap.
// `buy` and `sell` move a position BY an amount; they do not set it TO a side.
// So `sell(qty = lots)` while long one leaves the position flat and never
// short: a long only strategy wearing a short's clothing, which takes about
// half the trades and looks plausible in a report.
//
// `lots + abs(pos.size)` is the amount that covers what is open and opens the
// new side in one order. It is correct from flat too, where `pos.size` is zero
// and the order is simply the entry.
//
// One order rather than `close()` then `buy()` also matters at the broker: two
// orders are two lots of brokerage and two chances to be half filled. The
// engine still reports it as the two trades it is.
//
// THERE IS ALSO `order.reverse()`, AND IT IS NOT THE SAME CALL. It closes what
// is open and opens the same size the other way, as two orders. So:
//
//   order.reverse()                   two orders, new size = the size that was open
//   buy(qty = lots + abs(pos.size))   one order,  new size = lots
//
// They agree only while the open size is already `lots`. They part the moment
// it is not: after a pyramided entry, or after somebody changes the quantity
// input mid-run, `order.reverse()` carries the old size forward and this line
// does not. Neither is the right one in general. Pick the one whose sentence is
// the sentence you mean, and this script means "hold `lots`, whichever way".

atrLen = input(10, "ATR length", min = 1, max = 200)
factor = input(3.0, "Factor", min = 0.1, max = 20)
lots = input(1, "Quantity", min = 1)

strategy("Stop and reverse", overlay = true, qty = lots)

// supertrend answers two series: the band, and the direction. The direction is
// negative while the trend is up, which is the library's convention and not
// something this script decides.
s = supertrend(factor, atrLen)
band = s[0]
dir = s[1]
isLong = dir < 0

// The first bar has no previous direction to compare against, so it can flip
// neither way. Without this guard the run opens a position on bar zero
// whichever way the trend happened to start.
turnedUp = not bar.isFirst and isLong and not isLong[1]
turnedDown = not bar.isFirst and not isLong and isLong[1]

plot(bar.isFirst ? none : band, "Supertrend", isLong ? lime : red, width = 2)

if turnedUp
    buy(qty = lots + abs(pos.size), tag = "Up")

if turnedDown
    sell(qty = lots + abs(pos.size), tag = "Down")
